racture we see around the world but  also a chance for many countries to  suddenly have a say and have and play  their part now um and not just be the us  and the eu  or china and and whoever else  but we saw during the pandemic heaps of  countries piling into vietnam and  producing their  investors at davos told me that vietnam  is now full so we're seeing this  overspelling into cambodia and cambodia  is trying to present itself as a  transparent nation that will be a great  business partner for producers around  the world okay man well we've heard a  lot from the united states and i  mentioned comparing them you know we  talk about the west we talk about the  united states we talk about europe a lot  of the time but let's hear from a  european leader on this i mean  so  if we ask the question is a world of  regionalization localization and  nationalization a better world well for  the last word on that let's hear from  the german chancellor olaf shorts who  gave the final keynote at the uh world  economic forum this time around  more economic resilience is the order of  the day in this multi-polar crisis-prone  world  and here too the answer must be  diversification for politics and  business alike  at the same time we must be careful that  the necessary diversification does not  become a pretext for isolation  tariff barriers and protectionism to put  it bluntly de-globalization is the wrong  track it will not work  the wrong track is that the message that  you were hearing a lot from davos that  deglobalization was right you'd expect  it from there but is there any converts  that that was basically the message i  was getting along with a greener  globalization okay ben for zulin thank  you so much for joining us on this dw  business special looking at decoupling  and de globalization i would like to  thank my guest sir ben fazoulen here in  the studio with me and anahita toms  thank you so much for joining us from  the international law firm baker  mckenzie uh 